  "body": "What exactly is the author implying when setting the `muted` attribute on a `video` element?\r\n1. Video does not have an audio track.\r\n2. Video may have audio track, but author does not want the user to ever hear the audio track.\r\n3. Video may have an audio track, but author believes the track is not interesting to hear unless users chooses to. (e.g. track is noise, explicit, etc..)\r\n\r\nCurrently `muted` attribute on `video` is interpreted differently by iOS/Safari than other browsers. Most browsers interpret it as (3). They mute the video by default but still allow the user to unmute it later. iOS/Safari however seems to interpret is as either (1) or (2) and does NOT provide a way for the user to unmute a muted video in their HTML5 player implementation. This causes interesting problems specially now that muted autoplay is supported in mobile browsers. Because to get a video to autoplay, author must mark it as muted, but then user has no way of unmuting it in iOS/Safari.\r\n\r\nI believe the implications of `muted` attribute on UAs should be clarified better in the specification so we don't end up with this different UA behaviour.\r\n\r\nThoughts?\r\n\r\n@hober @domenic @mounirlamouri \r\n",
